  "problem_statement": "`soft_str` function incorrectly handles non-string inputs\n\nThe `soft_str` function is expected to convert non-string inputs to strings while leaving string inputs unchanged. However, a recent change has caused it to incorrectly handle non-string inputs, resulting in unexpected behavior.\n\nTo reproduce the issue, consider the following example:\n\n```python\nfrom markupsafe import soft_str, Markup\n\ndef test_soft_str_issue():\n    # This should return a string type\n    result1 = soft_str(15)\n    print(type(result1))\n    assert type(result1) is str, f\"Expected str, got {type(result1)}\"\n\n    # This should return a Markup type\n    result2 = soft_str(Markup())\n    print(type(result2))\n    assert type(result2) is Markup, f\"Expected Markup, got {type(result2)}\"\n```\n\nThe expected output should be:\n```\n<class 'str'>\n<class 'markupsafe.Markup'>\n```\n\nHowever, the actual output is different, indicating that the function does not convert non-string inputs to strings as expected. This issue affects the handling of various input types and needs to be addressed to ensure consistent behavior."
